(1)  629/     174 :                     	; This controls the update rate for the DAC...
(1)  630/     174 :                     	; My speculation for the rate at which the DAC updates:
(1)  631/     174 :                     	;	Z80 clock = 3.57954MHz = 3579540Hz (not sure?)
(1)  632/     174 :                     	;	zWaitLoop (immediately below) is waiting for someone to set
(1)  633/     174 :                     	;		'de' (length of DAC sample) to non-zero
(1)  634/     174 :                     
(1)  635/     174 :                     	; The DAC code is sync'ed with VBLANK somehow, but I haven't quite got that
(1)  636/     174 :                     	; one figure out yet ... tests on emulator seem to confirm it though.
(1)  637/     174 :                     	; Next, there are "djnz" loops which do busy-waits.  (Two of them, which is
(1)  638/     174 :                     	; where the '2' comes from in my guess equation.)  The DACMasterPlaylist
(1)  639/     174 :                     	; appears to use '1' as the lowest input value to these functions.  (Which,
(1)  640/     174 :                     	; given the djnz, would be the fastest possible value.)
(1)  641/     174 :                     	; The rate seems to be calculated by 3579540Hz / (60Hz + (speed * 4)) / 2
(1)  642/     174 :                     
(1)  643/     174 :                     	; This "waitLoop" waits if the DAC has no data, or else it drops out!
(1)  644/     174 :                     
(1)  645/     174 :                     ; zloc_174:
(1)  646/     174 :                     zWaitLoop:
(1)  647/     174 : 7A                  	ld	a,d			; 4
(1)  648/     175 : B3                  	or	e			; 4
(1)  649/     176 : 28 FC               	jr	z,zWaitLoop		; 7	; As long as 'de' (length of sample) = 0, wait...
(1)  650/     178 :                     
(1)  651/     178 :                     	; 'hl' is the pointer to the sample, 'de' is the length of the sample,
(1)  652/     178 :                     	; and 'iy' points to the translation table; let's go...
(1)  653/     178 :                     
(1)  654/     178 :                     	; The "djnz $" loops control the playback rate of the DAC
(1)  655/     178 :                     	; (the higher the 'b' value, the slower it will play)
(1)  656/     178 :                     
(1)  657/     178 :                     
(1)  658/     178 :                     	; As for the actual encoding of the data, it is described by jman2050:
(1)  659/     178 :                     
(1)  660/     178 :                     	; "As for how the data is compressed, lemme explain that real quick:
(1)  661/     178 :                     	; First, it is a lossy compression. So if you recompress a PCM sample this way,
(1)  662/     178 :                     	; you will lose precision in data. Anyway, what happens is that each compressed data
(1)  663/     178 :                     	; is separated into nybbles (1 4-bit section of a byte). This first nybble of data is
(1)  664/     178 :                     	; read, and used as an index to a table containing the following data:
(1)  665/     178 :                     	; 0,1,2,4,8,$10,$20,$40,$80,$FF,$FE,$FC,$F8,$F0,$E0,$C0."   [zDACDecodeTbl / zbyte_1B3]
(1)  666/     178 :                     	; "So if the nybble were equal to F, it'd extract $C0 from the table. If it were 8,
(1)  667/     178 :                     	; it would extract $80 from the table. ... Anyway, there is also another byte of data
(1)  668/     178 :                     	; that we'll call 'd'. At the start of decompression, d is $80. What happens is that d
(1)  669/     178 :                     	; is then added to the data extracted from the table using the nybble. So if the nybble
(1)  670/     178 :                     	; were 4, the 8 would be extracted from the table, then added to d, which is $80,
(1)  671/     178 :                     	; resulting in $88. This result is then put back into d, then fed into the YM2612 for
(1)  672/     178 :                     	; processing. Then the next nybble is read, the data is extracted from the table, then
(1)  673/     178 :                     	; is added to d (remember, d is now changed because of the previous operation), then is
(1)  674/     178 :                     	; put back into d, then is fed into the YM2612. This process is repeated until the number
(1)  675/     178 :                     	; of bytes as defined in the table above are read and decompressed."
(1)  676/     178 :                     
(1)  677/     178 :                     	; In our case, the so-called 'd' value is shadow register 'a'
(1)  678/     178 :                     
(1)  679/     178 :                     zWriteToDAC:
(1)  680/     178 : 10 FE               	djnz	$			; 8	; Busy wait for specific amount of time in 'b'
(1)  681/     17A :                     
(1)  682/     17A : F3                  	di				; 4	; disable interrupts (while updating DAC)
(1)  683/     17B : 3E 2A               	ld	a,2Ah			; 7	; DAC port
(1)  684/     17D : 32 00 40            	ld	(zYM2612_A0),a		; 13	; Set DAC port register
(1)  685/     180 : 7E                  	ld	a,(hl)			; 7	; Get next DAC byte
(1)  686/     181 : 07                  	rlca				; 4
(1)  687/     182 : 07                  	rlca				; 4
(1)  688/     183 : 07                  	rlca				; 4
(1)  689/     184 : 07                  	rlca				; 4
(1)  690/     185 : E6 0F               	and	0Fh			; 7	; UPPER 4-bit offset into zDACDecodeTbl
(1)  691/     187 : 32 8D 01            	ld	(.highnybble+2),a	; 13	; store into the instruction after .highnybble (self-modifying code)
(1)  692/     18A : 08                  	ex	af,af'			; 4	; shadow register 'a' is the 'd' value for 'jman2050' encoding
(1)  693/     18B :                     
